Environment Package Specification (EPS) v1.0

1. Purpose

The Environment Package Specification (EPS) defines a universal, portable, and secure format for creating, distributing, storing, and executing virtual environments within the Space Leaf ecosystem.

EPS enables interoperability between:

* Simulation environments
* AI agents
* Digital twins
* Educational worlds
* Training systems
* Creative experiences
* Research environments
* Future Holodeck-class systems

The specification is platform-agnostic and designed to support desktop, mobile, cloud, AR, VR, XR, and future immersive interfaces.

⸻

2. Core Design Principles

2.1 Portability

An EPS package shall function across compliant runtimes.

2.2 Modularity

Components must remain replaceable without rebuilding the entire environment.

2.3 Safety

All executable behaviors operate within defined permissions.

2.4 Extensibility

Future versions may add modules without breaking existing environments.

2.5 Human Oversight

Human users retain authority over environment execution and permissions.

⸻

3. Package Structure

Standard extension:

.eps

Expanded structure:

environment.eps

Contains:

/manifest
/assets
/scenes
/agents
/scripts
/simulation
/audio
/video
/textures
/models
/data
/localization
/security
/thumbnails
/extensions

⸻

4. Manifest

Every package must contain:

manifest.json

Example:

{
“eps_version”: “1.0”,
“environment_id”: “com.spaceleaf.mars.station”,
“name”: “Mars Research Station”,
“author”: “Space Leaf Corp”,
“description”: “Scientific research simulation.”,
“version”: “1.0.0”,
“license”: “SLC-Open”,
“created”: “2026-09-15”,
“minimum_runtime”: “1.0”,
“recommended_runtime”: “1.2”,
“dependencies”: [],
“permissions”: [],
“safety_profile”: “Educational”,
“signature”: “pending”
}

⸻

5. Environment Metadata

Metadata defines discoverability and classification.

{
“category”: “Education”,
“tags”: [
“science”,
“space”,
“research”
],
“estimated_size”: “4.2GB”,
“multiplayer_supported”: true,
“offline_supported”: true,
“recommended_age”: “13+”
}

⸻

6. Scene Definition

Scenes describe spatial environments.

scene.json

{
“scene_id”: “main_habitat”,
“name”: “Habitat Alpha”,
“entry_point”: true,
“gravity”: 3.7,
“lighting_profile”: “mars_day”,
“audio_profile”: “station_ambient”
}

Each environment may contain unlimited scenes.

⸻

7. Asset Registry

All assets must be registered.

asset_registry.json

{
“asset_id”: “chair_001”,
“type”: “model”,
“path”: “/models/chair.glb”,
“checksum”: “sha256”
}

Supported asset categories:

* Models
* Textures
* Materials
* Audio
* Video
* Documents
* Datasets
* Animations
* Physics Assets

⸻

8. Agent Framework

Agents operate as independent modules.

agent.json

{
“agent_id”: “engineering_assistant”,
“name”: “Engineering Assistant”,
“type”: “AI”,
“model_profile”: “engineering”,
“memory_scope”: “local”,
“permissions”: []
}

Agent classes:

* Instructor
* Guide
* Companion
* Analyst
* Engineer
* Researcher
* Moderator
* Narrator
* Custom

⸻

9. Simulation Module

Defines environmental behavior.

simulation.json

{
“physics_enabled”: true,
“weather_enabled”: false,
“economy_enabled”: false,
“life_support_enabled”: true
}

Optional systems:

* Physics
* Ecology
* Weather
* Economics
* Transportation
* Energy
* Manufacturing
* Population
* Custom

⸻

10. Security Framework

Security is mandatory.

Levels:

Level 0 - Static Environment

No scripts

Level 1 - Local Logic

Environment scripts only

Level 2 - Agent Interaction

AI enabled

Level 3 - Network Connected

External communication allowed

Level 4 - Enterprise

Advanced integrations

⸻

11. Permission System

Examples:

{
“permissions”: [
“voice_input”,
“local_storage”,
“multiplayer”,
“agent_execution”
]
}

Restricted permissions require approval.

Examples:

* external_network
* device_access
* financial_transactions
* code_execution

⸻

12. Safety Profiles

Profiles establish environment behavior.

Educational

Family

Professional

Research

Creative

Developer

Enterprise

Custom

Safety profiles may impose:

* Content restrictions
* Agent limitations
* Network restrictions
* Data retention policies

⸻

13. Localization

Supported languages stored within:

/localization

Example:

en-US.json
es-ES.json
fr-FR.json

⸻

14. Environment States

Save format:

state.epss

Contains:

* User position
* Object states
* Agent memory
* Simulation variables
* Permissions granted

⸻

15. Package Signing

All published environments should be signed.

Supported:

* SHA-256 integrity validation
* Public/private key signatures
* Publisher verification certificates

Unsigned packages trigger warnings.

⸻

16. Marketplace Compatibility

EPS packages shall support:

* Direct installation
* Repository deployment
* Enterprise deployment
* Offline transfer
* Educational distribution

⸻

17. Future Extensions

Reserved extension namespaces:

/quantum
/neural
/digital_twin
/robotics
/spatial_computing
/holographic
/biological_simulation

Unknown extensions must be ignored safely by compliant runtimes.

⸻

18. Compliance Levels

EPS-C1

Basic Environment

EPS-C2

Interactive Environment

EPS-C3

AI Environment

EPS-C4

Networked Environment

EPS-C5

Enterprise Simulation

EPS-C6

Holodeck-Class Environment

⸻

19. Holodeck-Class Requirements

A Holodeck-Class Environment must support:

* Dynamic scene generation
* Real-time AI interaction
* Multi-agent coordination
* Persistent memory
* User personalization
* Safety controls
* Environment modification during runtime
* Cross-device synchronization

⸻

20. Governance

The EPS standard shall be maintained through versioned releases.

Major Version:
Breaking changes

Minor Version:
Feature additions

Patch Version:
Corrections and clarifications

Example:

EPS 1.0.0
EPS 1.1.0
EPS 2.0.0

Backward compatibility should be preserved whenever practical.

End of Specification